text:
alex: quick check — I'm hitting repeated "Unexpected end of JSON input" in the Node TS SSE client when refreshToken kicks in mid-stream. stack shows JSON.parse on a truncated chunk.
maria: ugh — are you using fetch + ReadableStream or eventsource? any minimal repro?
alex: fetch + ReadableStream. simplified loop:
```ts
const resp = await fetch(url, {headers});
const reader = resp.body.getReader();
const decoder = new TextDecoder();
let buf = '';
while(true){
  const {value, done} = await reader.read();
  if(done) break;
  buf += decoder.decode(value, {stream: true});
  const lines = buf.split('\n');
  buf = lines.pop() || ''; // last chunk may be partial
  for(const l of lines){
    if(!l.trim()) continue;
    const ev = JSON.parse(l); // throws when l is split in middle
    handle(ev);
  }
}
```
maria: that approach is fine but token refresh sometimes stalls processing long enough that trailers arrive split. The split-brace issue is normal — keep incomplete tail in buf (you do) but the problem is when refresh path blocks parsing or reader backlog grows.
sam: we've seen similar: when refreshToken awaits a network call, the "read loop" is paused while awaiting? Node's async awaits won't block other microtasks but your code awaiting refresh inside the loop can cause scheduling that makes the server continue sending. This leads to very large buf and memory/backpressure issues.
phil: solution we used in the python client: decouple network read from JSON parsing. Read bytes into a small ring buffer, schedule parser tasks off the loop (worker or setImmediate), and signal server to slow down if queue exceeds threshold.
alex: thinking of two quick mitigations: 1) ensure refreshToken is non-blocking (use local cached jwt with background refresh and fallback) or 2) if refresh is unavoidable, pause the stream by closing reader and re-open with refreshed token (but that loses context).
maria: re-open loses KV cache; not ideal. Another idea: when buffer grows >128KB, emit a client-control event to server via a secondary control channel (or POST) with "client:pause" so server can throttle. Server already understands control events in our protocol (we have an opt-in control bus).
sam: can also limit parser work per tick: parse up to N lines then await setImmediate to allow refresh to finish, preventing long sync work. Also put a hard cap on buf length and emit an error if exceeded.
phil: for Node TS specifically, using TransformStream to chunk-by-line + a small synchronous parser yield works nicely. But still need backpressure hook.
alex: I opened a draft PR with a few changes: background refresh (try cached first), parser yield points, and a control pause when buffer >128KB. PR: https://github.com/redwood-inference/sdk/pull/432
maria: nice. can you add an e2e test that simulates token refresh taking ~500ms while server sends small fragments and ensures client doesn't OOM and resumes parsing after refresh?
alex: yes — adding test harness that injects artificial delay in auth endpoint and a fake SSE with split JSON tokens.
sam: also flagging an open question for infra: should server emit periodic "heartbeat" empty objects so clients can detect liveness and avoid interpreting small partial payloads as data?
phil: hearts are useful for long streams, but prefer protocol-level control events. server team ok with adding heartbeat if client advertises need.
deploy-bot: merge queued: PR 432 merged to staging — build green :white_check_mark:
maria: sweet. next steps: review the PR changes in main, add e2e, and follow up with infra about optional heartbeat/control channel. I'll assign owners. Thanks all.
